\section*{EXPTIME-completeness of variable-dimensional WL}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 133; 2026-09-25), the asserted result is that deciding joint-update WL equivalence with binary dimension is EXPTIME-complete even for connected simple uncolored subcubic graph pairs.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 133; 2026-09-25), homogeneous and affine block equations admit shared readouts and exact local extension. Channels and prefix forests convert the blocks to maximum degree three, while one common block offset supplies an entire announced bijection rather than only a point response.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 133; 2026-09-25).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 133; 2026-09-25), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: deciding joint-update WL equivalence with binary dimension is EXPTIME-complete even for connected simple uncolored subcubic graph pairs.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{ParityLifts.lean}, \texttt{VariableWL.lean}, \texttt{WLIdentification.lean}, \texttt{WeisfeilerLeman.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.ParityWL.parity\_reduction}, \texttt{OAI.VariableWL.main}, \texttt{OAI.WLIdentification.main}, \texttt{OAI.WLTime.unconditional\_time\_lower\_bound}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

